    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The only test class is `AcceptanceTest`, and both its tests pass with `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\n## Summary\n\nI converted owner deletion from a hard delete to a soft delete. The changes, each placed in the unit that owns that responsibility:\n\n1. **`Owner` entity** (`model/Owner.java`) \u2014 added a `deleted` field (`@Column(name = \"deleted\")`, `Boolean`, defaulting to `FALSE`) with `getDeleted`/`setDeleted`, plus a small `isDeleted()` helper that treats an absent flag as not-deleted. Because the field initializes to `FALSE`, a newly built owner is `deleted: false`.\n\n2. **`DeleteOwner` function** \u2014 instead of `ownerRepository.delete(owner)`, it now sets `deleted = true` and `save`s the owner, retaining the row. The DELETE pipeline still responds 204 (2xx).\n\n3. **`RequireUniqueIdentity` function** \u2014 the single duplicate/identity gate now skips any existing owner where `isDeleted()`, so a soft-deleted owner no longer blocks re-creation of an otherwise-duplicate owner.\n\n4. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `deleted` boolean to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` exposes it and MapStruct auto-maps it from the entity (GET and create responses now carry `deleted`).\n\n5. **Schema files** (`db/{h2,hsqldb,postgres,mysql}/schema.sql`) \u2014 added `deleted BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE` to the `owners` table.\n\nRepository reads (`findById`, `findAll`) were left unfiltered, so `GET /api/owners/{id}` still returns a soft-deleted owner (now with `deleted: true`), matching the spec and both acceptance tests.",
